## 5. Product Library and Product Roster

Build a central product library so product information is entered once and reused across multiple gift guides and sections.

### Product fields

Support the following fields where applicable:

- Stable product ID.
- Product name.
- Product URL.
- Product image URL.
- Price.
- Currency.
- Product type.
- Category and tags.
- Short description.
- Product features and verified specifications.
- Optional source notes.
- Creation and modification timestamps.

Allow users to create, edit, search, filter, and archive product records.

Provide practical import methods, including:

- Paste a list of products.
- Import CSV data.
- Add or edit individual products manually.

Validate URLs and prices where appropriate. Preserve original URLs and avoid silently changing product slugs.

For imported data, report invalid rows and missing fields clearly. Allow valid rows to be imported without discarding the entire batch.

Do not assume that an AI model can reliably scrape all Fourthwall product pages. Treat user-supplied product data as the source of truth.

### Product reuse

A product may appear in multiple sections of one guide or in several different guides.

Editing a shared product record should not silently overwrite guide-specific editorial copy.

Keep the distinction between:

- Canonical product information.
- Guide-specific presentation data.
- Section-specific display settings.

For example, a product may have a canonical name and price but use a shorter display title, different editorial description, or different badge in a particular gift guide.

Where possible, use stable product IDs rather than duplicated product objects.

## 6. Gift-Guide Project Management and Persistent Storage

Every gift guide must be a persistent, independently editable cloud project.

Examples:

- Greek Mythology Gifts.
- Gifts for History Lovers.
- Roman History Gifts.
- World War II Gifts.

Creating one guide must not overwrite another.

### Saving and reopening

The user must be able to:

1. Create a guide.
2. Add products and configure sections.
3. Save the guide.
4. Leave the application.
5. Return later and reopen the guide with all saved content intact.
6. Add new products or edit existing ones.
7. Preview and export the updated sections.

Persist all relevant data, including section order, product assignments, editorial copy, category configurations, FAQ content, schema settings, and manual edits.

Use explicit save feedback and a sensible autosave strategy, with a visible saved/saving/error status.

Handle concurrent edits and failed network requests safely. Do not report that data is saved until the write succeeds. Avoid overwriting newer server data with stale client state.

Support recovery from interrupted editing where practical.

### Backup and restore

Include JSON export and import for project backup and recovery.

The backup must preserve enough structured information to reconstruct the saved guide and its section configurations. Clearly distinguish a project backup from an HTML export.

Validate imported backups and warn before replacing existing data.

## 7. Reusable Guide Structure and Section Editor

A gift guide consists of independently configurable sections.

The system must support the following section types using the provided templates.

### A. Quick Bookmarks

Use the existing Table of Contents HTML structure.

The current reference contains eight navigation entries. Support eight configurable entries by default, while keeping the configuration flexible enough to accommodate other guide structures when necessary.

For every entry, allow editing of:

- Visible label.
- Anchor destination.
- Order.
- Optional icon or prefix text.
- Enabled/disabled status.

Generate matching anchors and navigation links. Validate duplicate IDs and missing destinations.

### B. Quick Picks

Use the existing four-card Quick Picks design as the default template.

Allow the user to configure:

- Product selection.
- Badge text.
- Display title.
- Supporting description.
- Price display.
- Image and product link.
- CTA label.
- Card order.

The number of cards must be configurable. Do not hardcode exactly four cards in the renderer.

### C. Editor's Pick

Use the existing dark Editor's Pick spotlight template.

Allow the user to select a featured product and edit:

- Kicker or badge.
- Heading.
- Description.
- Displayed price.
- CTA label.
- Product URL and image.

The section must be independently editable and exportable.

### D. Main Editorial Sections

This is the core of the application.

Allow users to create any number of product sections and name them according to the guide.

Examples:

- Part 1: History T-Shirts.
- Part 2: Hoodies and Sweatshirts.
- Part 3: Books and Journals.
- Ancient Rome.
- World War II.
- Medieval History.

Each section should have configurable:

- Section title.
- Optional subtitle or introduction.
- Chapter banner and kicker.
- Product assignments.
- Product ordering.
- Editorial card copy.
- Optional secondary product grids.
- Section-specific template settings.

Use the existing `sog-editorial-list`, `sog-ed-card`, and related classes for the long-form cards.

The reference uses a 310px sticky-image editorial card with an image, title, badge, price, description, specifications, tags, and CTA. Preserve that structure.

Allow the number of products to vary freely. A section may contain 3, 15, 20, 30, or more items. Do not hardcode a 14-product limit.

Implement efficient product management for large lists, including search, selection, reordering, and clear visual grouping.

### E. More Gifts Grids

Use the existing secondary three-column product-grid template.

Allow the user to add one or more grids within the relevant guide structure, select products, edit titles and descriptions, and control ordering.

Support variable item counts. Use the existing responsive CSS classes.

Prevent accidental duplication when moving products between sections, unless the user intentionally chooses to feature a product more than once.

### F. Category Hub

Use the existing deity hub as the structural reference, but make it topic-agnostic.

Greek mythology may use Greek gods. A history guide may use historical eras, civilizations, conflicts, or themes.

Allow the user to configure:

- Hub heading and introduction.
- Category name.
- Subtitle.
